<header><p class="st-kicker">colony · South America</p><h1 class="st-title">Viceroyalty of the Río de la Plata</h1><p class="st-meta">1776 CE – 1814 CE · capital Buenos Aires</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The last viceroyalty created by Spain in the Americas, ruled from Buenos Aires, whose crisis in 1810 began Argentina's independence.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>To counter Portuguese expansion and smuggling, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-iii-of-spain">Charles III</a> created the Viceroyalty of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rio-de-la-plata">Río de la Plata</a> in 1776, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buenos-aires">Buenos Aires</a> as its capital and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pedro-de-cevallos">Pedro de Cevallos</a> as first <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viceroy-of-india">viceroy</a>. It joined the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/governorate">governorates</a> of Buenos Aires, <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/republic-of-paraguay">Paraguay</a> and Tucumán with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuyo">Cuyo</a> and the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/royal-audiencia-of-charcas">Royal Audiencia of Charcas</a>, including the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/potosi">Potosí</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">Silver</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/potosi">Potosí</a> now flowed out through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buenos-aires">Buenos Aires</a>, which boomed after the free-trade regulation of 1778 opened it to direct trade with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>. Intendancies replaced the old <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/governorate">governorates</a> in 1782. Hides and salted meat were growing exports. In 1806 and 1807 British forces invaded Buenos Aires and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/montevideo">Montevideo</a>, and local militias under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/santiago-de-liniers">Santiago de Liniers</a> drove them out, boosting <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/criollo-people">Creole</a> confidence.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon's</a> capture of the Spanish king, the May Revolution of 1810 deposed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viceroy-of-india">viceroy</a> and created the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/united-provinces-of-the-rio-de-la-plata">United Provinces of the Río de la Plata</a>. <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/republic-of-paraguay">Paraguay</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/royal-audiencia-of-charcas">Upper Peru</a> broke away, and royalist <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/montevideo">Montevideo</a> fell in 1814.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/viceroyalty-of-the-rio-de-la-plata.jpg" alt="Viceroyalty of the Río de la Plata" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: previous version Ignaciogavira; current version HansenBCN, designs from SanchoPanzaXXI · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Spain_(1785%E2%80%931873,_1875%E2%80%931931).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1776 CE – 1814 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>colony</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>colonial</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Buenos Aires</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Pedro de Cevallos, Juan José de Vértiz, Santiago de Liniers</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Roman Catholicism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Spanish</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/argentina">Argentina</a>, <a href="/countries/bolivia">Bolivia</a>, <a href="/countries/paraguay">Paraguay</a>, <a href="/countries/uruguay">Uruguay</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Viceroyalty of the River Plate, Virreinato del Río de la Plata</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1795&amp;focus=viceroyalty-of-the-rio-de-la-plata" aria-label="Viceroyalty of the Río de la Plata on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="